  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday morning through next Saturday)\nIssued at 213 PM EDT Sun Jul 5 2026\n\nSummer-like heat and diurnally driven thunderstorm chances will \nprevail through the rest of this week.\n\nBroad troughing continues over the Mid-Atlantic region through the \nlong term period. The upper level ridge remains adjacent to our CWA, \nbut mostly to our south this week. Recent 00Z and 06Z model data \nshows a weak, closed upper level low that sets up just south of the \nOhio River Valley and remains anchored there for the first several \ndays of the extended period. This H250 low should eventually be \npulled back into the jetstream and the upper level pattern should \nstay zonal through the rest of the forecast period.\n\nMaximum heat index values will still be near or just above advisory \ncriteria for us this week. The locations most favored to meet or \nexceed 105 degrees are still across southeast Georgia. PoPs towards \nthe mid-week have trended downward due to the new positions of the \nupper level low. Though the better forcing for thunderstorms has \nshifted north, isolated thunderstorms are still possible across the \narea each day as long as convective temperatures are being met. \nDamaging winds, heavy rainfall, and frequent lightning persist as \nthe main hazards from this pulse-type convection.",
